<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Geoffrey of Monmouth</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1095–c. 1155</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Geoffrey of Monmouth was a Welsh-born cleric and writer whose History of the Kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> (c. 1136) presented a largely legendary history of Britain from the Trojan Brutus to the seventh century. It popularised the stories of King Arthur, Merlin and King Lear and had an enormous influence on medieval literature, although it was largely invented.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/geoffrey-of-monmouth.jpg" alt="Geoffrey of Monmouth" width="320" height="402" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Colin Cheesman · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Geoffrey_of_Monmouth_at_Tintern_Station_(geograph_6023609).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Medieval writers such as Gildas and <b>Geoffrey of Monmouth</b> looked back to the Roman period, and the province became central to British archaeology and historical writing from the 18th century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-britain">Roman Britain · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The English custom is the wassail, whose name was borrowed from the Old Norse salutation ves heill, &quot;be in good health&quot;, and is first recorded as a drinking formula around 1140, in <b>Geoffrey of Monmouth</b>.</blockquote><a href="/essays/midwinter">Midwinter</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Monmouth, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in Llandaff Cathedral, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.81,-2.72&amp;year=1134" aria-label="Monmouth on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4489 372 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Geoffrey of Monmouth"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4939 642h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4939 642h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.81,-2.72&amp;year=1134">What was here in 1134? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…offered me a very ancient book in the British tongue, which, in a continued regular story and elegant style, related the actions of them all, from Brutus the first king of the Britons, down to Cadwallader the son of Cadwallo.&#8221;</p><footer>History of the Kings of Britain, book 1, dedication &#183; tr. Aaron Thompson, rev. J. A. Giles, 1848 &#183; the book no one else ever saw, his claimed source &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Six_Old_English_Chronicles/Geoffrey%27s_British_History/Book_1" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;And even the renowned king Arthur himself was mortally wounded; and being carried thence to the isle of Avallon to be cured of his wounds…&#8221;</p><footer>History of the Kings of Britain, book 11 (the battle of Camlann) &#183; tr. Aaron Thompson, rev. J. A. Giles, 1848 &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Six_Old_English_Chronicles/Geoffrey%27s_British_History/Book_11" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 535 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
